    "content": "\"The Big Dance\" is a popular nickname for the annual NCAA Division I men's basketball tournament, also known as March Madness. It is a 68-team, single-elimination tournament held in the United States to determine the national champion. The term was coined in 1977 by former Marquette coach Al McGuire, referencing the need to wear a \"blue blazer\" to \"the big dance\". Key 2026 Tournament Details: Selection Sunday: March 15, 2026. First Four: March 17–18, 2026. First Round: March 19–20, 2026. Final Four: April 4 & 6, 2026, in Indianapolis, Indiana. This video explains why the tournament is so popular:53sThe Craig Carton ShowYouTube • Mar 15, 2026 53s Origin of the Term:Former Marquette coach Al McGuire coined the phrase in 1977. When asked about wearing a lucky blazer to the tournament, he replied, \"Absolutely. You gotta wear the blue blazer when you go to the big dance,\" referring to the NCAA tournament as the ultimate event in college basketball. Key 2026 Tourney Info: The tournament field comprises 68 teams, including automatic qualifiers from conferences and at-large bids. The \"First Four\" games involve the final four at-large teams and the lowest-seeded conference champions fighting to enter the round of 64. The Women's NCAA tournament also features a \"Big Dance,\" with rounds leading to their national championship.",
